          "original": "programs, which he had carefully written out on paper because he wasn't comfortable with computers. I don't know how many other preteen boys sat in their grandfather's room, being taught how to simplify arithmetic expressionsand type them correctly into a com puter, but I remember doing that. I don't remember what the cal culations were all about, and I don't think I had a single clue about what I really did when I did it, but I was there, helping him. It probably took us much longer than it would have taken him alone, but who knows? I grew comfortable with the keyboard, something my grandfather never did. I would do this after school, or whenever my mother dropped me off at my grandparents' apartment. And I started reading the manuals for the computer, typing in the example programs. There were examples of simple games Linus Torva/ds and David Diamond 7",
